{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q004_ferritin_like_functional_divergence","answer":"SCOPe groups ferritins, ribonucleotide-reductase-like proteins, and manganese catalases into distinct families within the ferritin-like superfamily (a.25.1), in the ferritin-like fold (a.25) of the all-alpha class. Their shared architecture includes a characteristic four-helix bundle associated with metal binding. Superfamily membership supports remote common ancestry despite substantial functional divergence; shared fold or class alone would provide weaker evidence and would not establish homology.

Their different chemistries reflect specialization of this scaffold. Ferritins typically oxidize iron and store it as a mineral inside an oligomeric cage, although ferroxidase activity varies among ferritin subunits. Ribonucleotide-reductase-like proteins include the class I RNR small subunit, whose metal cofactor supports radical generation or initiation for nucleotide reduction by the separate catalytic large subunit. This does not mean that every protein in the RNR-like family is a nucleotide reductase. Manganese catalases use a binuclear manganese center to disproportionate hydrogen peroxide into water and oxygen.

Consequently, neither superfamily membership nor structural resemblance justifies transferring exact activity, substrate specificity, metal identity, radical mechanism, or cage assembly between these families. Even family membership may not establish these properties for an uncharacterized protein. Functional assignment requires corroborating sequence and active-site conservation, biological context, biochemical activity measurements, and, where relevant, metal analysis or spectroscopy. A remote family representative can support modeling of the conserved structural core, but detailed active-site or oligomeric modeling should use a template matched to the target’s family, ligand environment, and assembly. One representative cannot adequately capture the superfamily’s functional diversity.","evidence_used":["pretrained model knowledge"]}]}
